 "intro": "Masada, a rock fortress built by Herod above the Dead Sea, 73-74 AD. The last Jewish rebels hold out there. You are Lucius Flavius Silva, governor of Judaea, with the Tenth Legion.",
 "debrief": "In Josephus’s account, Silva walled Masada in with camps and a circumvallation, whose remains survive, then built a huge ramp up the western spur. His iron-clad tower and ram broke the stone wall, but the defenders built a wall of timber and earth behind it, which the Romans burned. The next morning, Josephus says, the Romans found the defenders had killed themselves; seven women and children survived. Historians debate parts of his account. The other choices are what-ifs.",
